these are the faces of those on the NHS  front line their commitment to keeping  us safe captured on canvas by dozens of  artists including Alistair Adams he's  painted countless actors and politicians  his work of Tony Blair famously hangs in  the National Portrait Gallery but at a  dark time Aleister wanted to honor those  not usually in the spotlight  I don't know Deborah terribly well at  all and so as a result what I'm playing  and painting really I suppose is the  notion of somebody that's kind of  upstanding and forthright and I'm  holding the line the person is there but  because we don't know the person  necessarily very well we're sort of like  using her as a metaphor for the greater  sort of sense of the time really despite  having never met Michele produced a  picture of Paula a nurse working in  neuro intensive care she's got a three  year old son and a five month old  daughter I think that's right and her  son when she opened it when she opened  the package her son that she said he's  mummy so when she told me that I thought  oh well I've definitely done a good job  the virtual exhibition is both beautiful  and moving particularly for those in  them Stan works as a theatre technician  in hospitals across the Midlands he was  painted by his friend that portrait to  me it will be a keepsake and it will  always remind you know myself my kids  even the grandchildren because it holds  history behind it and it shows how the  Solidarity which the NHS staff has gone  through and over the past 10 days or  months  traditionally portraits were seen as a  symbol of status the artists say these  paintings immortalize our heroes for  generations to come  it's hoped when life returns to normal  an exhibition will be open to celebrate  the portraits so the sacrifice of the  people in them is never forgotten a  Shannara nags Sky News
 

